1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,455
You bought your first crypto.

2
00:00:01,455 --> 00:00:03,702
You can see the balance on the screen.

3
00:00:03,702 --> 00:00:06,081
But who is actually holding it?

4
00:00:06,081 --> 00:00:07,312
Because on most platforms,

5
00:00:07,312 --> 00:00:09,391
the answer is not you.

6
00:00:09,391 --> 00:00:10,514
Stay to the end

7
00:00:10,514 --> 00:00:12,122
and you will know which of the two kinds

8
00:00:12,122 --> 00:00:12,734
you are using,

9
00:00:12,734 --> 00:00:14,022
and the one thing to do

10
00:00:14,022 --> 00:00:16,208
the moment a trade settles.

11
00:00:16,208 --> 00:00:17,268
Start with the number

12
00:00:17,268 --> 00:00:19,281
that surprises people most.

13
00:00:19,281 --> 00:00:20,930
Zero.

14
00:00:20,930 --> 00:00:23,169
That is how many private keys you hold

15
00:00:23,169 --> 00:00:24,068
when your coins sit

16
00:00:24,068 --> 00:00:26,060
on a centralized exchange.

17
00:00:26,060 --> 00:00:28,576
The company holds every one of them.

18
00:00:28,576 --> 00:00:31,313
So what is an exchange, actually?

19
00:00:31,313 --> 00:00:33,263
An exchange is a marketplace.

20
00:00:33,263 --> 00:00:35,285
Nothing more mysterious than that.

21
00:00:35,285 --> 00:00:37,254
It matches somebody who wants to buy

22
00:00:37,254 --> 00:00:39,080
with somebody who wants to sell,

23
00:00:39,080 --> 00:00:40,203
and takes a small cut

24
00:00:40,203 --> 00:00:41,867
for standing in the middle.

25
00:00:41,867 --> 00:00:43,788
It is also where the price comes from,

26
00:00:43,788 --> 00:00:45,416
and where liquidity lives.

27
00:00:45,416 --> 00:00:46,645
Liquidity just means

28
00:00:46,645 --> 00:00:48,412
there are enough buyers and sellers

29
00:00:48,412 --> 00:00:49,609
that your order fills

30
00:00:49,609 --> 00:00:52,317
at roughly the price you expected.

31
00:00:52,317 --> 00:00:53,679
But how that middle is built

32
00:00:53,679 --> 00:00:56,037
is where the two families split.

33
00:00:56,037 --> 00:00:57,454
One of them is a company.

34
00:00:57,454 --> 00:00:59,760
The other is a piece of code.

35
00:00:59,760 --> 00:01:00,977
And that single difference

36
00:01:00,977 --> 00:01:03,351
decides everything else about them.

37
00:01:03,351 --> 00:01:06,228
A company, or a smart contract?

38
00:01:06,228 --> 00:01:08,789
A centralized exchange is a business.

39
00:01:08,789 --> 00:01:09,415
Coinbase.

40
00:01:09,415 --> 00:01:10,168
Binance.

41
00:01:10,168 --> 00:01:12,028
Crypto.com.

42
00:01:12,028 --> 00:01:13,391
It runs an order book,

43
00:01:13,391 --> 00:01:14,604
it checks who you are,

44
00:01:14,604 --> 00:01:15,551
and it keeps your coins

45
00:01:15,551 --> 00:01:17,567
in wallets that it controls.

46
00:01:17,567 --> 00:01:19,226
A decentralized exchange

47
00:01:19,226 --> 00:01:21,158
has no company in it at all.

48
00:01:21,158 --> 00:01:22,545
You connect your own wallet,

49
00:01:22,545 --> 00:01:24,528
a smart contract does the swap,

50
00:01:24,528 --> 00:01:26,839
and your keys never leave your hands.

51
00:01:26,839 --> 00:01:29,394
Most of them do not even run an order book.

52
00:01:29,394 --> 00:01:30,969
You trade against a pool of coins

53
00:01:30,969 --> 00:01:32,347
that other users put there,

54
00:01:32,347 --> 00:01:34,755
and those users take the fees.

55
00:01:34,755 --> 00:01:35,882
So look at what each one

56
00:01:35,882 --> 00:01:37,739
actually hands you.

57
00:01:37,739 --> 00:01:38,698
Centralized.

58
00:01:38,698 --> 00:01:40,701
Deep liquidity and fast fills.

59
00:01:40,701 --> 00:01:42,320
Cash in, and cash out.

60
00:01:42,320 --> 00:01:43,625
It holds your keys.

61
00:01:43,625 --> 00:01:44,658
Decentralized.

62
00:01:44,658 --> 00:01:45,740
You keep your keys.

63
00:01:45,740 --> 00:01:47,130
No identity check.

64
00:01:47,130 --> 00:01:49,476
Thinner markets, and network fees.

65
00:01:49,476 --> 00:01:51,246
Neither column is the winner.

66
00:01:51,246 --> 00:01:51,969
They are answers

67
00:01:51,969 --> 00:01:54,061
to two different questions.

68
00:01:54,061 --> 00:01:56,178
What is a trading pair?

69
00:01:56,178 --> 00:01:58,012
One thing confuses every beginner,

70
00:01:58,012 --> 00:02:00,436
and it is written on every screen.

71
00:02:00,436 --> 00:02:03,756
You will see it written as BTC / USD.

72
00:02:03,756 --> 00:02:05,650
The first one is what you are buying.

73
00:02:05,650 --> 00:02:08,052
The second is what you are paying with.

74
00:02:08,052 --> 00:02:09,741
That is the whole idea.

75
00:02:09,741 --> 00:02:11,218
A price only means something

76
00:02:11,218 --> 00:02:13,178
measured against something else.

77
00:02:13,178 --> 00:02:15,476
And they come in three kinds.

78
00:02:15,476 --> 00:02:17,063
Crypto against real money.

79
00:02:17,063 --> 00:02:19,066
Crypto against other crypto.

80
00:02:19,066 --> 00:02:21,645
Crypto against a stablecoin.

81
00:02:21,645 --> 00:02:23,324
Which pairs a platform offers

82
00:02:23,324 --> 00:02:25,130
decides what you can buy directly,

83
00:02:25,130 --> 00:02:27,000
and what costs you three trades

84
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:28,845
and three sets of fees.

85
00:02:28,845 --> 00:02:31,555
Not your keys, not your coins

86
00:02:31,555 --> 00:02:34,318
Now the part that catches people out.

87
00:02:34,318 --> 00:02:35,598
The friendlier platform

88
00:02:35,598 --> 00:02:37,780
is the one where you own nothing.

89
00:02:37,780 --> 00:02:40,225
A centralized exchange feels safe.

90
00:02:40,225 --> 00:02:41,524
There is a support line.

91
00:02:41,524 --> 00:02:42,887
There is a password reset.

92
00:02:42,887 --> 00:02:45,112
It looks like online banking.

93
00:02:45,112 --> 00:02:46,866
But those are exactly the features

94
00:02:46,866 --> 00:02:48,328
that require somebody else

95
00:02:48,328 --> 00:02:50,128
to be holding the keys.

96
00:02:50,128 --> 00:02:51,326
Your balance is a row

97
00:02:51,326 --> 00:02:53,151
in that company's database.

98
00:02:53,151 --> 00:02:55,327
The coins behind it sit in their wallets,

99
00:02:55,327 --> 00:02:57,645
pooled with everybody else's.

100
00:02:57,645 --> 00:02:59,372
There is an old line about this

101
00:02:59,372 --> 00:03:01,268
and it has outlasted every exchange

102
00:03:01,268 --> 00:03:02,880
that ignored it.

103
00:03:02,880 --> 00:03:06,889
Not your keys, not your coins.

104
00:03:06,889 --> 00:03:08,268
So do not treat an exchange

105
00:03:08,268 --> 00:03:09,482
like a bank account.

106
00:03:09,482 --> 00:03:11,650
It is a place you pass through.

107
00:03:11,650 --> 00:03:13,940
So how do you choose one?

108
00:03:13,940 --> 00:03:15,254
Before you trust any platform

109
00:03:15,254 --> 00:03:16,473
with a single coin,

110
00:03:16,473 --> 00:03:18,799
run the same five checks.

111
00:03:18,799 --> 00:03:21,213
Two-factor authentication from an app.

112
00:03:21,213 --> 00:03:24,298
Most coins held offline, in cold storage.

113
00:03:24,298 --> 00:03:26,237
A licence in a country you have heard of.

114
00:03:26,237 --> 00:03:28,560
Fees you can find without hunting.

115
00:03:28,560 --> 00:03:30,996
A history you can look up.

116
00:03:30,996 --> 00:03:31,784
Miss one of those

117
00:03:31,784 --> 00:03:33,715
and you are not choosing an exchange.

118
00:03:33,715 --> 00:03:35,351
You are hoping.

119
00:03:35,351 --> 00:03:36,417
And once you are in,

120
00:03:36,417 --> 00:03:38,840
four habits do most of the work.

121
00:03:38,840 --> 00:03:40,738
Turn the two-factor on first.

122
00:03:40,738 --> 00:03:43,083
Start with an amount you can afford to lose.

123
00:03:43,083 --> 00:03:45,727
Learn limit orders before market orders.

124
00:03:45,727 --> 00:03:47,203
Then move what you are not trading

125
00:03:47,203 --> 00:03:48,836
off the platform.

126
00:03:48,836 --> 00:03:50,922
A limit order waits for your price.

127
00:03:50,922 --> 00:03:53,227
A market order takes whatever is there.

128
00:03:53,227 --> 00:03:54,791
That difference costs beginners

129
00:03:54,791 --> 00:03:56,454
more than the fees do.

130
00:03:56,454 --> 00:03:59,200
So — back to the balance on your screen.

131
00:03:59,200 --> 00:04:00,284
The question was never

132
00:04:00,284 --> 00:04:02,161
which exchange is the best one.

133
00:04:02,161 --> 00:04:06,512
It is: who is holding it right now?

134
00:04:06,512 --> 00:04:07,207
Know that,

135
00:04:07,207 --> 00:04:08,025
and you already know

136
00:04:08,025 --> 00:04:10,200
whether it should still be sitting there.

137
00:04:10,200 --> 00:04:12,347
The full comparison and the platform reviews

138
00:04:12,347 --> 00:04:13,655
are linked below —

139
00:04:13,655 --> 00:04:16,350
and nothing here is financial advice.

140
00:04:16,350 --> 00:04:17,461
If that was useful,

141
